2.36.m:
Standing at the beginning, he should practice hitting the target situated square;
immediately after that, [the target that is] sharp, and that which is situated turning away.
Then he should practice the raised target at the end of the elevated mark, [and] the tuṣyaka;
then, with the bow placed in these target positions,
and with relaxed [fingers] placed on the arm-guard, he should shoot even with the hands [themselves].
In that target, O Rāma, two targets are known as 'firm';
know two targets to be 'puṣkara', and likewise two to be 'citrapuṣkara'.
The square and the sharp are declared to be the firm targets;
the square, the broad-sharp […].
The low [target] is designated as puṣkara, as is the target that is situated high.
The lower targets are in the puṣkara, while [the other two] are designated as citrapuṣkara.
Having thus made a group of targets on the right and on the other side,
the steadfast man, having conquered the target, should then first mount [a vehicle].
This very rule has been declared, seen by practitioners there;

2.37.m:
a man, taking a club in his hand from the right thumb,
raising it, should strike firmly at the head of that named [target];
he should cause its downfall with both hands.

This model, accompanied by rule, is beneficial and a true release for pupils, provided by me whose mind seeks your welfare;
for the sake of enduring the fatigue of battle for mounts, it is to be struck down first by the wise man as worthy of training.
Puṣkara said:
Whirling, whirling upward, tossed, leaped, issued forth, sped,
fallen, raised up, and unagitated army-fall;
striking upward, holding down, and left and right,
unseen, sun-burst, terrible, and kingly wide-mouthed;
deformed, falling away, terrifying, fearful,
complete or third, foot-by-foot, lotus-born, and enemy-born;
pratyālīḍha, anālīḍha, and boar's wallow—
thus thirty-two sword-movements for holding a shield are to be known.
Straight, extended, broad, and bent sideways:
five movements of the dart in single combat have been indicated by the great-souled ones.
Severing, threatening, dropping, revolving, and whirling, / as well as slicing, cutting, and wheel-whirling;

3.1.m:
Both [kinds] are to be known by the nature of prose and verse, endowed with their characteristics;
and verse there is of two kinds: regular metre and free-formed metre.
There are twenty-one nominal case-endings; a word is held to end in either nominal or verbal endings.
The first person is one, and the second is considered the middle [person];
and the third is called the highest: this triad expresses their action.
There are nine verbal terminations called active voice,

The nominal stem is one's own base here, and that base they also wish to be the verbal root;
by this all literature is composed, whether prose or together with verse.
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
With a quarter of six syllables, the metre is called Gāyatrī;
Uṣṇih has seven syllables, and Anuṣṭubh has eight syllables;
Bṛhatī has nine syllables, and that designated Paṅkti has ten syllables;
Triṣṭubh has eleven syllables, and Jagatī has twelve syllables.
That with thirteen syllables is to be known with the prefix 'ati-' likewise;
and Śakvarī has fourteen syllables, O delight of the Yadus.
Atiśakvarī is declared to have fifteen syllables;
they recognise the sixteen-syllabled metre as that which is recorded as Aṣṭi.
The metre named Atyaṣṭi has seventeen syllables;
and that with eighteen syllables is proclaimed as named Dhṛti.
Likewise, that named Atidhṛti has twenty minus one;
and twenty syllables make that named Kṛti, O best of the Yadus.

Twenty joined with one is declared to be Prakṛti;
and twenty-two is likewise declared to be named Ākṛti.
Twenty-three is Vikṛti, and that with one [more] is considered Saṃkṛti;
and Saṃkṛti joined with one is declared to be Atikṛti, O king.
And Utkṛti of twenty-six [syllables] is the metre spoken of by the wise.
One should designate these respectively by their four quarters.
A light syllable is of one measure, marked with a clear stroke;
it should be placed so, while a heavy [syllable] has two measures and should be curved.
Even a light syllable which is final becomes heavy, O king;
and long and protracted [syllables] are likewise called heavy.
A light syllable before which conjunct consonants follow is considered heavy.
By this distinction of light and heavy syllables, the permutation [is formed], O Yādava.
All the varieties of metres arise in many ways, O king;
I shall explain how they are generated, listen to that:

3.2.m:
Being distinct and varied in region after region, it cannot be fully stated through grammatical rules; / that which is called corrupt (apabhraṣṭa) in the worlds is to be understood as the domain of one who knows the region.
3.8
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Devas, divaukasas, divyas, gīrvāṇas, tridaśas, suras [are synonyms for the gods]; / Hari, Janārdana, Viṣṇu, Vāsudeva, and Adhokṣaja;
Nārāyaṇa, Hṛṣīkeśa, the wielder of the discus, he whose mount is Garuḍa; / Bala, Saṃkarṣaṇa, Ananta, Śeṣa, he who has the palmyra on his banner, the plowman;
Pradyumna, Kāmadeva, the archer, he whose banner bears the makara; / Aniruddha whose weapon is the sword, and likewise he whose banner bears the ṛṣya deer;
Lakṣmī, Karīṣiṇī, Śrī, and also the goddess Vibhāvarī; / the discus is called Sudarśana, and the bow of Viṣṇu is Śārṅga;
The bow of Śiva is named Pināka; that belonging to Varuṇa is Gāṇḍīva; / the bow of Śakra is declared to be Ājagava; the vajra is called the kuliśa;
The mace of Viṣṇu is declared to be Kaumodakī, O king; / the plow is called Lalāma, and the pestle is remembered as Sānanda;

Pāñcajanya is remembered as the conch which Janārdana bears; / the lotus in his hand is mentioned, and likewise that called Astrālokya;
Bhūmi, Vasundharā, Pṛthvī, Kṣamā, Kṣoṇī, Dharā, Mahī [for the earth]; / nabhas, kha, gagana, and likewise ākāśa, the stride of Hari [for the sky];
Tārkṣya, Suparṇa, Garuḍa, the mount and emblem on the banner of Hari; / the serpent Ananta, the bearer of the plow, Śeṣa, he whose banner bears the palmyra, the plowman;
Brahmā, the Grandfather, Śambhu, the Self-born, Creator of Beings; / Mahādeva, Bhava, Śambhu, Tryambaka, the husband of Pārvatī;
Umā, Satī, she who was born of the mountain, and likewise she who was born of Menakā; / Bhadrakālī is remembered as Durgā, and the gaṇas are remembered as the Pramathas;
